body:
Potentially related to https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/101452, note this comment: https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/101452#issuecomment-2657817175

Because, again, Windows + Nvidia.

@LiveTrower -- Could you force Godot over OpenGL3 and try again? Launch it from a command line prompt with the command line option: ```--rendering-driver opengl3```

> When people say it "freezes" what exactly do they mean? Does the demo hang indefinitely, is it just for a few seconds? Do you have to kill the process?
>
@m4rr5 I mean that the load reaches 100% but it freezes and never responds. The same thing happens if I run the scene directly. On the other hand, what @Joy-less mentioned also occurs, where it reaches 75% and never responds, but that only happens when it has to recompile the pipelines. However, if you wait a few seconds, then kill the process, and finally run it again, it will load normally up to 100%, but the aforementioned issue will still happen. The problem mentioned by @Joy-less has been happening to me since versions prior to v4.4.beta3, but the issue I am reporting now does not occur in that case. I hope I have explained myself clearly, as it seems that all these problems are different.

@smedelyan did you try my earlier suggested workaround, which is to set the worker thread pool size to a large number. As I mentioned here, there are tradeoffs:

> 1000 isn’t ideal, as the `WorkerThreadPool` preallocates 1000 threads. Each thread consumes 1MB of stack space (by default) on Windows, so those 1000 threads will consume a minimum of 1GB of virtual memory. You can probably find a smaller number, like 50 or 20, that’s suitable for now.

Perhaps this might help you work around the issue.
